    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm trying to set up some reorder logic for our lithium-ion battery components. We're getting a lot of variability in lead times from our Shenzhen suppliers lately, so I need a way to calculate a reliable reorder point that accounts for that uncertainty so we don't hit stockouts during our peak season. Can you give me a standard formula or some logic for calculating safety stock and the reorder point? I have the average daily usage and the lead time in days, but the lead time is the part that's all over the place.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A reliable reorder point, when dealing with unpredictable lead times, is best calculated using a safety stock buffer. You need to calculate the safety stock based on the variability of your demand *and* the variability of your lead time. The standard formula is: Reorder Point = (Average Daily Usage $\\times$ Average Lead Time) + Safety Stock.\n\nFor the safety stock, you'll need to use the standard deviation of demand and the standard deviation of lead time, incorporating a desired service level (Z-score). This helps you buffer against the worst-case scenario, preventing stockouts when things get chaotic.\n\nA simplified approach, if you can't easily calculate the combined standard deviation, is to look at the maximum expected lead time and use that in your base calculation, then add a safety stock based on the historical maximum demand during that extended period. This is less statistically rigorous but might be easier for your current setup.\n\nRemember, the goal is to keep enough stock to cover the *uncertainty* of when the next shipment will arrive, not just the average arrival time. It's a balancing act between holding costs and the risk of a stockout, which is what the safety stock manages.\n\nIt's highly",
      "source": "target",
